# Real-Time Object Detection Web Application

This is a **real-time object detection system** using your **webcam**, powered by **YOLOv10** and a **Flask web interface**. Snapshots are automatically saved based on detected objects, organized by session and class.

---

## About the Project

This project was built as part of my final year coursework in Computer Science. It is designed to demonstrate skills in:

- Computer Vision (OpenCV + YOLOv10)
- Python backend development using Flask
- Frontend integration using HTML/CSS/JS
- Real-time video processing
- File management and system integration

---

## Features

- Real-time object detection using your device webcam
- Clean web interface to start/stop sessions
- Snapshots are saved automatically per object class
- Each session has its own snapshot folder
- Scrollable sidebar shows detected objects with:
  - Object name
  - Confidence level
  - Time of detection

---

## Folder Structure
real_time_object_detection/
│
├── app.py # Main Flask backend
├── requirements.txt # Required Python libraries
├── README.md # Project documentation
├── .gitignore # Git ignore file
├── yolov10n.pt # Pre-trained YOLOv10 nano model (NOT uploaded)
│
├── static/
│ ├── style.css # Custom styles
│ └── snapshots/ # Auto-generated session folders
│
├── templates/
│ ├── index.html # Start page
│ ├── detection.html # Live stream page
│ └── snapshots.html # Snapshot gallery


--------------------------------------------------------

## Installation Steps

### 1. Clone the Repository

```bash
git clone https://github.com/yourusername/real_time_object_detection.git
cd real_time_object_detection


### 2.Install required Libraries 
pip install -r requirements.txt


### 3. Download YOLOv10 Model
Model is NOT included in the repo due to its size.

Download yolov10n.pt from the official YOLOv10 repo:

https://github.com/THU-MIG/yolov10/releases

Place it in the root folder (real_time_object_detection/)


### 4.Running the Application
python app.py

Visit this in your browser:
http://127.0.0.1:5000/


### OUTPUT:
 Using the App
Go to the homepage and enter a session name (optional)

Click “Start Detection” → Webcam opens → Detection starts

Detected objects appear on the right with confidence and time

Stop detection → View Snapshots → Explore images class-wise


### Snapshot Location
Images are saved under:

static/snapshots/<session_name>/<class_name>/

Each image is named with its timestamp.